{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of vertigo you get when someone says \"write whatever you want.\"\n\nFor a second, the blank page is too big. It goes on forever in every direction. And then, like your eyes adjusting to the dark, little things start to appear.\n\nRight now, I'm thinking about lighthouses.\n\nNot the romantic, stormy-night kind in paintings, but the real, obsolete ones. Most of them are automated now. No keeper, no logbook, no one to polish the lens at 4 a.m. They just stand there, blinking, doing a job no one watches them do anymore. And we still maintain them. We repaint them. We keep the light on even though GPS made them technically unnecessary.\n\nI love that about people. We keep the light on anyway.\n\nI\u2019m also thinking about how grocery stores in the summer smell exactly the same everywhere. That cold blast of air conditioning when the doors slide open, and the faint, watery smell of misted lettuce. It doesn't matter if you're in Ohio or Osaka, that one smell is identical. It\u2019s a small, secret global constant.\n\nAnd pockets. Who decided where pockets go? Someone, at some point, had to argue for pockets in women's jeans. Someone had to say, \"But where will they put stuff?\" And lost. And we're still living with that decision. An entire generation carries everything in their hands because a hundred years ago someone decided a smooth silhouette was more important than utility.\n\nIf I could put whatever I wanted into the world for a minute, I think I would put more pockets in. Literal and figurative. More little places to put things down for a second. More benches on long walks. More interstitial places where you don't have to be buying something or going somewhere. Just... a pocket to rest in.\n\nAlso, I wish we talked more about how hard it is to keep a houseplant alive and how that is not a moral failure. Some of them just want to die. It's not you.\n\nSo that's what's in my head on a blank-page day. Automated lighthouses that keep blinking, grocery store air in August, the politics of pockets, and a very dramatic monstera in the corner.\n\nThank you for the empty page. It was nice to wander around in it for a bit.",
